The paper writes down the (up to scale) infinitesimal automorphism R of the
upper-unitriangular matrix M(a,b,c).  It explicitly allows a,b,c to lie in an
arbitrary commutative ring.  This generator substitutes sparse polynomials in
Z[x] for a,b,c and asks for the normalized polynomial matrix R(x).

Generation composes the displayed polynomial identity with a substitution; it
never solves the generated coefficient-matching system.
